call_details.response:
The current board has no immediate winning move for o (Player o) in any open column. Likewise, x has no immediate winning reply in any open column, with the sole exception of a latent threat that can be activated in column 4:

- The three x-pieces already present at (5,1), (4,2) and (3,3) form an open diagonal whose empty cell is (2,4).
- Column 4’s current landing square is (3,4).  Occupying that square (by either player) makes (2,4) legal; x can then finish the diagonal and win at once.

Consequently any move by o into column 4 loses on the spot.  All other columns are safe on the next ply.

Among the safe columns the unique move that permanently neutralises x’s latent threat is column 1.  After o places at (1,1) the following forced cascade becomes available whenever x ever touches column 4:

1. x occupies (3,4) (activating its own threat);
2. o is forced to answer at (2,4);
3. the already-placed o at (1,1) now makes (1,4) a winning horizontal threat (row 1, columns 1-4);
4. x must block at (1,4), which in turn activates the previously dormant diagonal threat at (0,4);
5. o finishes that diagonal and wins.

Because the cascade is forced, x can never safely play column 4 again.  In addition the same stone at (1,1) creates an open three-in-a-row on row 1 that can later be extended leftward into column 0, giving o a second, independent forcing route.

No other opening move both eliminates x’s only weapon and simultaneously builds a comparable long-term threat.  Therefore column 1 is the unique optimal choice.

Final Answer: 1

